66
00:04:24.601 --> 00:04:26.755
Scale for this design (assumptions).

67
00:04:27.035 --> 00:04:28.401
Here is a quick table:

68
00:04:28.681 --> 00:04:30.003
Quantity, and Value.

69
00:04:30.283 --> 00:04:31.094
Cities:

70
00:04:31.374 --> 00:04:33.763
100 (India + international).

71
00:04:34.043 --> 00:04:35.046
Trips per day:

72
00:04:35.326 --> 00:04:44.777
5M; peak ~250 trips per second city-wide totals, ~2,000 requests per second including re-requests and fare checks.

73
00:04:45.057 --> 00:04:46.593
Drivers online at peak:

74
00:04:46.873 --> 00:04:48.046
600k.

75
00:04:48.326 --> 00:04:49.990
Driver location updates:

76
00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:56.158
600k drivers ÷ 4 s about 150k updates per second.

77
00:04:56.438 --> 00:05:02.497
Fare estimates (where to? typing): ~20k per second at peak (many estimates per trip).

78
00:05:02.777 --> 00:05:07.385
Trip events: ~20 state events per trip, then 100M per day.

79
00:05:08.735 --> 00:05:10.121
Estimation highlights:

80
00:05:10.401 --> 00:05:12.961
Location updates dominate write volume.

81
00:05:13.241 --> 00:05:17.828
They are ephemeral: keep the latest position in memory and the history in the lake.

82
00:05:18.108 --> 00:05:20.732
Fare estimates dominate read computation.

83
00:05:21.012 --> 00:05:23.337
Each needs a route, an ETA and a price.

84
00:05:23.617 --> 00:05:29.953
Matching is CPU-bound per area, not throughput-bound globally, so it is partitioned by geography.

85
00:05:31.303 --> 00:05:32.178
Part 2.

86
00:05:32.458 --> 00:05:33.418
Requirements.

87
00:05:34.668 --> 00:05:35.521
Functional.

88
00:05:35.801 --> 00:05:47.428
Riders set pickup and destination, see fare and ETA per product (auto, bike, mini, sedan, premium, shared), request, track, pay (cash, UPI, cards, wallet), rate and tip.

89
00:05:47.708 --> 00:05:59.143
Drivers go online and offline, receive and accept or decline offers, navigate, start and end trips, collect cash or see digital payment, and see earnings, incentives and payouts.

90
00:05:59.423 --> 00:06:05.588
Dynamic pricing within regulatory limits (VERIFY per jurisdiction); promotions and passes.

91
00:06:06.938 --> 00:06:11.951
Scheduled rides, airport and railway-station queues, rentals (hourly), intercity.

92
00:06:12.231 --> 00:06:14.215
Shared rides (pooling) where offered.

93
00:06:14.495 --> 00:06:21.237
Safety features: verified identity, masked numbers, trip sharing, SOS, ride check-ins on anomalies.

94
00:06:21.517 --> 00:06:24.866
Support for disputes, refunds, lost items, incidents.

95
00:06:26.216 --> 00:06:27.325
Non-functional.

96
00:06:27.605 --> 00:06:28.971
Here is a quick table:

97
00:06:29.251 --> 00:06:30.616
Attribute, and Target.

98
00:06:30.896 --> 00:06:31.813
Latency:

99
00:06:32.093 --> 00:06:40.840
Fare estimate p95 300 ms; a match decision within a few seconds of the request; location to rider screen 2 s.

100
00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.187
Availability:

101
00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:51.597
Request, match and trip ≥ 99.99% per city; a trip in progress must survive any single service failure.

102
00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:52.795
Correctness:

103
00:06:53.075 --> 00:07:00.968
A driver is never assigned two trips at once (unless pooled); every trip is billed exactly once; driver earnings are exact.

104
00:07:01.248 --> 00:07:02.123
Fairness:

105
00:07:02.403 --> 00:07:07.117
Transparent fares; no discrimination in matching; consistent surge rules.

106
00:07:07.397 --> 00:07:08.315
Privacy:

107
00:07:08.595 --> 00:07:12.797
Location data minimised and access-controlled; phone numbers masked.

108
00:07:13.077 --> 00:07:13.931
Safety:

109
00:07:14.211 --> 00:07:18.456
SOS reaches responders within seconds; incident data preserved.

110
00:07:19.806 --> 00:07:20.702
Part 3.

111
00:07:20.982 --> 00:07:22.518
High-Level Architecture.

112
00:07:23.768 --> 00:07:25.112
Here is the diagram.

113
00:07:28.962 --> 00:07:30.626
Key architectural decisions:

114
00:07:31.976 --> 00:07:33.341
Here is a quick table:

115
00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:35.392
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

116
00:07:35.672 --> 00:07:36.589
Partitioning.

117
00:07:36.869 --> 00:07:37.701
Choice:

118
00:07:37.981 --> 00:07:45.299
Marketplace services partitioned by city, then geo cells; cities are the unit of deployment and blast radius.

119
00:07:45.579 --> 00:07:46.261
Why:

120
00:07:46.541 --> 00:07:49.784
Supply and demand are local, and failures stay local.

121
00:07:50.064 --> 00:07:51.259
Location state.

122
00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:52.371
Choice:

123
00:07:52.651 --> 00:07:57.259
In-memory, sharded by cell; latest position only; history to the stream.

124
00:07:57.539 --> 00:07:58.221
Why:

125
00:07:58.501 --> 00:08:04.517
150k writes per second of ephemeral data (Ch 47 L1, L2).

126
00:08:04.797 --> 00:08:05.715
Trip state.

127
00:08:05.995 --> 00:08:06.827
Choice:

128
00:08:07.107 --> 00:08:12.013
Durable, strongly consistent per trip; a workflow engine for long-running steps.

129
00:08:12.293 --> 00:08:12.976
Why:

130
00:08:13.256 --> 00:08:15.069
Money and safety depend on it.

131
00:08:15.349 --> 00:08:16.821
Communication with apps.

132
00:08:17.101 --> 00:08:17.933
Choice:

133
00:08:18.213 --> 00:08:27.365
Persistent connections (WebSocket or MQTT or gRPC streams) for offers and live updates; push notifications as fallback.

134
00:08:27.645 --> 00:08:28.328
Why:

135
00:08:28.608 --> 00:08:30.272
Offers must arrive in 1 s.

136
00:08:31.622 --> 00:08:32.859
Geography model.

137
00:08:33.139 --> 00:08:33.971
Choice:

138
00:08:34.251 --> 00:08:36.811
Hexagonal hierarchical cells (H3).

139
00:08:37.091 --> 00:08:37.774
Why:

140
00:08:38.054 --> 00:08:42.875
Uniform neighbours, multi-resolution aggregation (§5.1).

141
00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:44.414
Pricing and matching.

142
00:08:44.694 --> 00:08:45.526
Choice:

143
00:08:45.806 --> 00:08:49.731
Separate engines sharing forecasts; ML-driven with guard rails.

144
00:08:50.011 --> 00:08:50.694
Why:

145
00:08:50.974 --> 00:08:52.787
Different time scales and owners.

146
00:08:54.137 --> 00:08:55.055
Part 4.

147
00:08:55.335 --> 00:08:57.212
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

148
00:08:58.462 --> 00:09:01.555
Journey 1: a rider books a sedan in Bengaluru.

149
00:09:01.835 --> 00:09:02.774
Open the app.

150
00:09:03.054 --> 00:09:12.697
The app sends its location and receives nearby cars (for display, with coarse and jittered positions to protect driver privacy) and each product's ETA.

151
00:09:12.977 --> 00:09:19.057
These come from the geo index: a count and nearest-driver query in the rider's cell and its neighbours.

152
00:09:19.337 --> 00:09:20.787
Choose the destination.

153
00:09:21.067 --> 00:09:29.622
As the rider types, the place search service autocompletes: a places index with popularity and personalisation (Ch 42).

154
00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:37.027
The pickup point is snapped to a safe, accessible spot: a gate or the correct side of the road, learned from past pickups.

155
00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:39.977
Fare estimate per product:

156
00:09:40.257 --> 00:09:49.452
Route and ETA: the maps service computes the route and trip ETA, a routing engine plus an ML correction for traffic (§6.3).

157
00:09:49.732 --> 00:10:02.724
Upfront fare: pricing computes it from the base fare, per-km and per-minute components, estimated tolls and airport fees, and the dynamic multiplier for the pickup area and time (§6.4).

158
00:10:03.004 --> 00:10:04.924
It applies promotions and taxes.

159
00:10:06.274 --> 00:10:10.178
Fare quote: the fare is shown with a quote ID valid for a few minutes.

160
00:10:10.458 --> 00:10:15.898
The quote is stored so the trip is billed at the quoted price, unless the route changes materially.

161
00:10:16.178 --> 00:10:17.053
Request.

162
00:10:17.333 --> 00:10:25.013
The trip service creates a trip in state REQUESTED, holding the quote, rider, pickup, destination, product and payment method.

163
00:10:25.293 --> 00:10:30.221
An idempotency key prevents duplicate trips from double taps (Ch 20).

164
00:10:30.501 --> 00:10:36.538
For digital payment, a small pre-authorisation or payment method check may run (VERIFY per method).

165
00:10:37.888 --> 00:10:38.720
Matching.

166
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.709
The request joins the matching batch for its area.

167
00:10:41.989 --> 00:10:51.845
Every few seconds the matcher solves an assignment across all open requests and available drivers (§6.2), and chooses this rider's driver.

168
00:10:52.125 --> 00:11:00.061
Driver lock: the chosen driver is locked with a conditional update (available, then offered), so no other request can take them.

169
00:11:00.341 --> 00:11:01.045
Offer.

170
00:11:01.325 --> 00:11:12.035
The offer goes to the driver's app over the persistent connection, showing pickup distance, rider rating and estimated earnings (VERIFY what regulations require drivers see).

171
00:11:12.315 --> 00:11:15.152
The driver has ~10 to 15 s to accept.

172
00:11:16.502 --> 00:11:23.158
Decline or timeout: the driver is released, and the request re-enters the next batch with a priority boost.

173
00:11:23.438 --> 00:11:24.377
Accepted.

174
00:11:24.657 --> 00:11:26.513
The trip becomes DRIVERASSIGNED.

175
00:11:26.793 --> 00:11:31.571
The rider sees the driver's name, photo, vehicle and plate, and the live position.

176
00:11:31.851 --> 00:11:34.795
The driver's app switches to navigation to the pickup.

177
00:11:35.075 --> 00:11:38.105
Masked calling and chat are enabled between the two.

178
00:11:38.385 --> 00:11:39.259
Arriving.

179
00:11:39.539 --> 00:11:45.086
Geofence detection marks ARRIVED when the driver is within ~50 m of the pickup.

180
00:11:46.436 --> 00:11:51.577
A waiting timer starts: free waiting, then a per-minute charge, VERIFY local rules.

181
00:11:51.857 --> 00:11:52.625
Start.

182
00:11:52.905 --> 00:11:56.105
The rider shares a 4-digit PIN or OTP.

183
00:11:56.385 --> 00:11:59.223
The driver enters it, and the trip becomes ONTRIP.

184
00:11:59.503 --> 00:12:01.337
This prevents wrong-rider pickups.

185
00:12:01.617 --> 00:12:02.641
During the trip:

186
00:12:02.921 --> 00:12:09.833
Locations: the driver's positions are map-matched and stream to the rider's app and trusted contacts (if shared).

187
00:12:10.113 --> 00:12:18.135
Safety monitoring: watches for long unexpected stops, route deviation and possible crashes (§5.9).

188
00:12:19.485 --> 00:12:24.669
Destination change: the rider can change the destination, which gets a new fare quote.

189
00:12:24.949 --> 00:12:25.631
End.

190
00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:30.050
The driver ends the trip at the destination and the trip becomes COMPLETED.

191
00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:42.319
The final fare is computed: the upfront fare if the route matched;. adjusted if there were stops, a destination change, significant detours, waiting or tolls (§5.6).

192
00:12:42.599 --> 00:12:43.389
Payment:

193
00:12:44.739 --> 00:12:50.072
Digital: charged via the payment method (UPI collect or autopay, card, wallet).

194
00:12:50.352 --> 00:12:54.576
Cash: the driver's app shows the amount, and the driver confirms receipt.

195
00:12:54.856 --> 00:12:58.675
The commission owed by the driver is debited from their earnings balance.

196
00:12:58.955 --> 00:13:07.104
Ledger: the rider's charge, the driver's earnings, commission, taxes and promotions are all posted (§5.7).

197
00:13:07.384 --> 00:13:08.365
Afterwards:

198
00:13:09.715 --> 00:13:11.763
Rating: both sides rate each other.

199
00:13:12.043 --> 00:13:17.974
Ratings feed quality systems (with bias controls), and tips go 100% to the driver.

200
00:13:18.254 --> 00:13:20.366
Receipt: emailed and shown in the app.

201
00:13:20.646 --> 00:13:24.763
The trip data is final, and disputes open a support case referencing it.

202
00:13:26.113 --> 00:13:27.927
Journey 2: a driver's day.

203
00:13:28.207 --> 00:13:44.697
Going online: the driver passes periodic selfie identity checks (face match against the onboarded photo; VERIFY consent and regulations);. document validity is checked (licence, permit, insurance, fitness certificate);. the driver goes ONLINE.

204
00:13:44.977 --> 00:13:45.895
Positioning:

205
00:13:47.245 --> 00:14:01.943
the app shows a demand heat map (forecast demand versus supply) and active incentives, for example, complete 12 trips between 7 to 11 am for 300 rupeesextra;. drivers may move to busier areas.

206
00:14:02.223 --> 00:14:04.485
Offers: offers arrive while online.

207
00:14:04.765 --> 00:14:07.581
The acceptance rate and cancellation rate are tracked.

208
00:14:07.861 --> 00:14:13.386
Excessive declines may reduce priority, a policy decision with fairness considerations.

209
00:14:13.666 --> 00:14:21.282
Destination mode: the driver can set a home direction, and matching then prefers trips heading that way (limited uses per day).

210
00:14:22.632 --> 00:14:40.829
Earnings: each trip's earnings (fare minus commission, plus tips and incentives) appear in real time;. cash collected is netted against amounts owed to the platform;. the driver balance may go negative with heavy cash trips, in which case the driver must top up or earnings are deducted.

211
00:14:41.109 --> 00:14:50.709
Payouts go to the driver's bank daily or weekly, with instant cash-out options (a payment rail such as IMPS or UPI; VERIFY) (I03).

212
00:14:52.059 --> 00:14:58.075
Going offline: fatigue rules may force breaks after N hours online (VERIFY local rules).

213
00:14:59.425 --> 00:15:02.220
Journey 3: a surge at 6 pm in the rain.

214
00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:05.785
The spike: at 5:45 pm rain starts.

215
00:15:06.065 --> 00:15:14.855
Requests in the business district's cells jump 3 times within minutes, while supply there stays flat, as drivers avoid rain or are stuck in traffic.

216
00:15:15.135 --> 00:15:31.135
Forecasting: the real-time demand signal (app opens, fare checks, requests, unfulfilled requests per cell per minute) and the supply signal (open drivers, drivers finishing trips nearby) feed the forecaster;. it predicts the next 15 to 30 minutes.

217
00:15:32.485 --> 00:15:41.210
Pricing computes a multiplier per area that balances expected demand with available and attracted supply (§6.4).

218
00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:57.831
Within guard rails: smoothing over time;. caps (including regulatory caps; VERIFY);. consistency between neighbouring areas;. no surges during declared emergencies (a policy decision; many platforms cap pricing in emergencies, VERIFY).

219
00:15:58.111 --> 00:15:58.943
Effects:

220
00:16:00.293 --> 00:16:10.576
some riders wait or choose cheaper products;. drivers see the surge on the heat map and move in;. wait times recover;. the multiplier decays as balance returns.

221
00:16:10.856 --> 00:16:16.552
Measurement: request completion rate, pickup ETAs and rider cancellations are measured per area.

222
00:16:16.832 --> 00:16:23.253
Experiments test pricing policies by region-time units, not by user (§7.5).

223
00:16:24.603 --> 00:16:26.353
Journey 4: airport pickup.

224
00:16:26.633 --> 00:16:30.345
Arrival: the airport is a geofenced zone with a driver queue.

225
00:16:30.625 --> 00:16:34.593
Drivers entering the holding area join a FIFO queue per product.

226
00:16:34.873 --> 00:16:37.347
Request: a rider requests from the terminal.

227
00:16:37.627 --> 00:16:45.457
The pickup point is fixed (a designated bay), and the first eligible driver in the queue is offered the trip, not the nearest one.

228
00:16:45.737 --> 00:16:48.681
Fairness: drivers who leave the zone lose their place.

229
00:16:48.961 --> 00:16:57.835
Short trips may give the driver a return to queue priority to avoid penalising them (a policy choice; VERIFY local airport rules and fees).

230
00:16:58.115 --> 00:17:01.443
Fees: airport fees are added to the fare as regulated.

231
00:17:02.793 --> 00:17:04.543
Journey 5: a shared ride.

232
00:17:04.823 --> 00:17:10.049
Request: a rider requests shared and gets a lower fare with a wider arrival window.

233
00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:25.156
Insertion: the pooling engine tries to insert the pickup and drop-off into existing trips' routes, where the added detour for existing riders is within limits (for example, + 8 min) and seats are available (§6.6).

234
00:17:25.436 --> 00:17:30.663
Update: if inserted, the existing riders' ETAs update and the driver's route re-plans.

235
00:17:30.943 --> 00:17:35.252
If not, a new pooled trip starts with this rider as the first passenger.

236
00:17:35.532 --> 00:17:37.495
Fares are fixed upfront per rider.

237
00:17:37.775 --> 00:17:44.431
A pool that doesn't materialise still charges the shared fare, which is the incentive for accepting uncertainty.

238
00:17:45.781 --> 00:17:47.829
Journey 6: a safety incident.

239
00:17:48.109 --> 00:17:54.594
Detection: during a night trip, the car stops for 12 minutes at an unscheduled location away from the route.

240
00:17:54.874 --> 00:18:01.999
The safety monitor detects a long stop plus a route deviation, and triggers a ride check push to rider and driver:

241
00:18:02.279 --> 00:18:03.645
Is everything OK?

242
00:18:03.925 --> 00:18:06.506
Escalation: the rider presses SOS.

243
00:18:06.786 --> 00:18:20.013
The app: calls the emergency number (112 in India; VERIFY integration requirements);. shares the live location and trip details with the platform's safety team and the rider's trusted contacts.

244
00:18:21.363 --> 00:18:28.317
Response: the safety operations console shows a timeline of the trip (locations, events, driver history).

245
00:18:28.597 --> 00:18:32.800
Agents follow a playbook, and the event data is preserved (legal hold).

246
00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:37.133
After the trip: an investigation, and account actions per policy.

247
00:18:37.413 --> 00:18:42.149
Data is shared with law enforcement only under lawful process (VERIFY).

248
00:18:43.499 --> 00:18:44.459
Part 5.

249
00:18:44.739 --> 00:18:46.275
Core Subsystems in Depth.

250
00:18:47.525 --> 00:18:50.107
Geospatial model, hexagonal cells.

251
00:18:50.387 --> 00:19:14.259
H3, Uber's open-source hierarchical hexagonal grid (publicly described; VERIFY), divides the earth into hexagons at 16 resolutions: at resolution 9, a hexagon is ~0.1 km² (edge ~170 m), good for local supply counts;. at resolution 7 (~5 km²), good for pricing areas.

252
00:19:14.539 --> 00:19:24.459
Why hexagons: every neighbour is the same distance away (squares have diagonal neighbours at a different distance), so k-ring neighbourhoods approximate circles well.

253
00:19:24.739 --> 00:19:26.957
Aggregation up the hierarchy is cheap.

254
00:19:27.237 --> 00:19:28.091
Uses:

255
00:19:29.441 --> 00:19:42.091
live supply per cell;. demand per cell;. pricing zones (groups of cells);. heat maps;. geofences (airports, restricted areas, as sets of cells plus exact polygons);. analytics.

256
00:19:43.441 --> 00:19:46.492
Location ingestion and the live supply index.

257
00:19:46.772 --> 00:19:56.372
Ingestion: driver apps send GPS every 1 to 4 s (adaptive by speed and trip state) over the persistent connection to location gateways.

258
00:19:56.652 --> 00:20:03.799
Gateways validate the points, filtering impossible jumps and spoofing indicators (§5.10).

259
00:20:04.079 --> 00:20:11.311
Map matching (§6.5) snaps points to road segments, which gives heading and road position for ETA.

260
00:20:11.591 --> 00:20:21.297
The live index is sharded by city and cell, in memory: cell, then drivers (id, status, product, position, heading, last seen), updated on each ping;.

261
00:20:22.647 --> 00:20:30.711
drivers moving between cells are moved between buckets;. drivers not seen for N seconds are marked stale and excluded from matching.

262
00:20:30.991 --> 00:20:34.063
Replication: replicas per shard give failover.

263
00:20:34.343 --> 00:20:40.317
A process restart rebuilds from the next pings in seconds, because the state is ephemeral by design.

264
00:20:40.597 --> 00:20:49.365
History: every ping goes to Kafka, then the lake (for ETA training, safety investigations and disputes), with retention and access limits.

265
00:20:49.645 --> 00:20:56.279
Details and measured trade-offs of geo indexes and ingestion are in Ch 47 L1, L2.

266
00:20:57.629 --> 00:20:58.739
Maps platform.

267
00:20:59.019 --> 00:21:16.299
Road graph: road segments with attributes (one-way, turn restrictions, speed classes, U-turn permissions), from licensed or open map data (OpenStreetMap plus a commercial provider) and corrected by driver trace mining (new roads, closures, wrong turn restrictions).

268
00:21:16.579 --> 00:21:18.435
Routing: shortest-time paths.

269
00:21:18.715 --> 00:21:28.912
At city scale, contraction hierarchies or similar precomputation make queries milliseconds, instead of Dijkstra over millions of nodes (§6.3).

270
00:21:30.262 --> 00:21:40.971
Traffic: live segment speeds are computed from driver traces, a stream aggregation of speeds per segment per minute, and blended with historical profiles by time of day.

271
00:21:41.251 --> 00:21:45.198
ETA service: route time from the graph, then an ML correction model.

272
00:21:45.478 --> 00:21:50.363
Uber has published work on deep-learning ETA post-processing (VERIFY DeepETA).

273
00:21:50.643 --> 00:21:56.723
Pickup points: clustering historical pickup locations per place to suggest the best pickup spots.

274
00:21:58.073 --> 00:21:59.033
Forecasting.

275
00:21:59.313 --> 00:22:06.503
What is predicted: demand (requests) and supply (drivers available) per cell group for the next 5 to 60 minutes.

276
00:22:06.783 --> 00:22:15.977
Features: recent history, time of day and week, weather, events (concerts, cricket matches), holidays, airport flight arrivals and train arrivals.

277
00:22:16.257 --> 00:22:23.575
Models: gradient-boosted trees or neural sequence models, with separate short-horizon and longer-horizon models.

278
00:22:23.855 --> 00:22:34.436
Consumers: pricing (§6.4), incentives design, heat maps, and matching look-ahead, which avoids sending the last driver out of an area about to be busy.

279
00:22:35.786 --> 00:22:37.130
Matching and dispatch.

280
00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:38.370
Why batch?

281
00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:44.090
Assigning each request immediately to its nearest driver is greedy and makes the city worse.

282
00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:49.191
Example: a driver 2 minutes from rider A and 3 minutes from rider B gets A.

283
00:22:49.471 --> 00:22:57.151
B then waits 9 minutes for the next driver, when giving the driver to B and another driver to A would have made both waits 4 minutes.

284
00:22:57.431 --> 00:23:11.234
The batch: every 2 to 5 seconds per area, the matcher collects open requests and available drivers (including those about to finish trips nearby) and solves a minimum-cost bipartite matching (§6.2).

285
00:23:12.584 --> 00:23:23.976
The cost: pickup ETA (from maps), plus penalties such as heading, driver's destination mode, product compatibility, driver fairness and rider priority after previous declines.

286
00:23:24.256 --> 00:23:31.275
Constraints: product (auto versus sedan), accessibility, EV range, airport queue rules, driver preferences.

287
00:23:31.555 --> 00:23:33.859
After solving, dispatch sends offers.

288
00:23:34.139 --> 00:23:37.104
Acceptance and timeouts feed back into the next batch.

289
00:23:37.384 --> 00:23:38.408
Consistency:

290
00:23:39.758 --> 00:23:58.190
driver state transitions (available, then offered, then assigned) are compare-and-set operations on the driver's state, so two matchers can never assign one driver twice;. matchers are partitioned by area, with cross-boundary drivers handled by the area owning the request (with neighbour reads).

291
00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:02.079
Pricing: upfront fares and final fares.

292
00:24:02.359 --> 00:24:15.607
The upfront fare is quoted before the request: base + per-km times route distance + per-min times route time + fees + tolls, times dynamic multiplier, minus promotions, + taxes.

293
00:24:15.887 --> 00:24:17.444
It is rounded per the rules.

294
00:24:17.724 --> 00:24:19.495
The quote is stored with its inputs.

295
00:24:19.775 --> 00:24:27.988
The final fare equals the quote, unless: the destination changed (re-quote);. stops were added;. waiting exceeded the free period;.

296
00:24:29.338 --> 00:24:34.074
tolls differed;. the actual route diverged significantly because of the rider.

297
00:24:34.354 --> 00:24:41.117
A reconciliation job compares the actual trip with the quote, and support rules auto-resolve common disputes.

298
00:24:41.397 --> 00:24:54.751
Regulatory limits: in India, the aggregator guidelines set rules on fare structure and on surge relative to base fares, and states may notify their own fares (VERIFY the current guidelines and state rules).

299
00:24:55.031 --> 00:24:59.362
The pricing engine enforces per-jurisdiction caps as hard constraints.

300
00:25:00.712 --> 00:25:04.232
Promotions: rider discounts, passes and subscriptions.

301
00:25:04.512 --> 00:25:07.968
Driver-side incentives are separate (§5.8).

302
00:25:09.318 --> 00:25:11.067
Payments, ledger and payouts.

303
00:25:11.347 --> 00:25:12.414
Rider payments:

304
00:25:12.694 --> 00:25:29.590
UPI (collect, intent or autopay mandates), cards (tokenised), wallets, corporate accounts, cash;. payment happens after the trip, with retries and fallback to a secondary method;. unpaid balances block the next request until settled (Ch 45).

305
00:25:29.870 --> 00:25:37.102
The ledger: double-entry accounts for rider payable, driver earnings, platform commission, taxes, promotions and tips.

306
00:25:38.452 --> 00:25:42.633
Cash trips: the platform's commission becomes a receivable from the driver.

307
00:25:42.913 --> 00:25:46.689
Digital trips: the driver's share becomes a payable to the driver.

308
00:25:46.969 --> 00:25:55.737
Payouts: scheduled or instant transfers to drivers' bank accounts, with daily reconciliation against bank statements (I03).

309
00:25:56.017 --> 00:25:56.764
Tax:

310
00:25:57.044 --> 00:26:05.172
GST on the platform's services and TDS or TCS obligations for drivers' earnings as applicable (VERIFY).

311
00:26:06.522 --> 00:26:07.482
Incentives.

312
00:26:07.762 --> 00:26:18.066
Types: quests (N trips in a window, then bonus);. area boosts (extra per trip in a zone and time);. guarantees (minimum hourly earnings when online in a zone).

313
00:26:18.346 --> 00:26:24.106
Design: incentives are budgeted by the forecast supply gap, with targeting by driver segment.

314
00:26:24.386 --> 00:26:27.906
Uplift is measured by experiments against a control group.

315
00:26:29.256 --> 00:26:34.035
Accounting: incentive payouts are ledger entries with the same rigour as fares.

316
00:26:35.385 --> 00:26:36.622
Safety platform.

317
00:26:36.902 --> 00:26:45.265
Onboarding checks: identity and document verification, background verification where required (VERIFY rules), vehicle checks.

318
00:26:45.545 --> 00:26:51.753
In-trip features: a PIN to start, masked calls, share trip, SOS, and ride check on anomalies.

319
00:26:52.033 --> 00:27:00.651
Anomaly detection: long unexpected stops;. route deviation (distance from the planned route beyond a threshold for a sustained time);.

320
00:27:02.001 --> 00:27:10.129
possible crash (sudden deceleration from phone sensors followed by a stop);. unexpected trip end far from the destination.

321
00:27:10.409 --> 00:27:13.012
Each has thresholds tuned to limit false alarms.

322
00:27:13.292 --> 00:27:19.799
Safety operations: a 24 times 7 team with prioritised queues and trip replay tools.

323
00:27:20.079 --> 00:27:21.487
Evidence is preserved.

324
00:27:21.767 --> 00:27:26.396
Women-safety and night features in some markets (VERIFY features by market).

325
00:27:27.746 --> 00:27:28.834
Fraud and risk.

326
00:27:29.114 --> 00:27:30.479
Here is a quick table:

327
00:27:30.759 --> 00:27:32.615
Fraud, and Signal, and Response.

328
00:27:32.895 --> 00:27:36.181
GPS spoofing (fake location apps, emulators).

329
00:27:36.461 --> 00:27:37.229
Signal:

330
00:27:37.509 --> 00:27:44.357
Mock-location flags, impossible speeds, inconsistent sensors, cell tower versus GPS mismatch.

331
00:27:44.637 --> 00:27:45.554
Response:

332
00:27:45.834 --> 00:27:47.626
Block trips, suspend accounts.

333
00:27:47.906 --> 00:27:50.957
Collusion (driver and rider fake trips for incentives).

334
00:27:51.237 --> 00:27:52.005
Signal:

335
00:27:52.285 --> 00:27:56.957
Repeated pairs, short trips with no real movement, shared devices or payment.

336
00:27:57.237 --> 00:27:58.154
Response:

337
00:27:58.434 --> 00:28:00.034
Withhold incentives, ban.

338
00:28:00.314 --> 00:28:01.402
Payment fraud.

339
00:28:01.682 --> 00:28:02.450
Signal:

340
00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:06.165
Stolen cards, chargebacks, unpaid cash balances.

341
00:28:06.445 --> 00:28:07.362
Response:

342
00:28:07.642 --> 00:28:10.373
Risk scoring at request, payment method checks.

343
00:28:10.653 --> 00:28:11.762
Promo abuse.

344
00:28:12.042 --> 00:28:12.810
Signal:

345
00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:15.735
Many new accounts on one device or payment.

346
00:28:16.015 --> 00:28:16.933
Response:

347
00:28:17.213 --> 00:28:19.559
Device fingerprinting, graph analysis.

348
00:28:20.909 --> 00:28:22.595
Fare inflation by drivers.

349
00:28:22.875 --> 00:28:23.643
Signal:

350
00:28:23.923 --> 00:28:25.971
Detours, waiting manipulation.

351
00:28:26.251 --> 00:28:27.168
Response:

352
00:28:27.448 --> 00:28:29.944
Route versus plan checks, auto refunds.

353
00:28:31.294 --> 00:28:32.233
Part 6.

354
00:28:32.513 --> 00:28:33.473
Algorithms.

355
00:28:34.723 --> 00:28:43.299
Nearest-driver search. k-ring search on H3: look up the rider's cell, then rings 1, 2, 3... until enough candidates are found.

356
00:28:43.579 --> 00:28:50.363
Compute exact straight-line distance, then route ETA for the top candidates only, because ETA is expensive.

357
00:28:50.643 --> 00:28:57.213
Alternatives: geohash prefix search, quadtrees, R-trees; compared in Ch 47 L1.

358
00:28:58.563 --> 00:28:59.694
Batched matching.

359
00:28:59.974 --> 00:29:08.529
Model: a bipartite graph of requests times drivers, with edges where the pickup ETA is under a maximum and constraints are satisfied.

360
00:29:08.809 --> 00:29:11.027
The edge cost is ETA plus penalties.

361
00:29:11.307 --> 00:29:22.934
Solving: the Hungarian algorithm (O(n³)) for small batches;. for larger batches, min-cost flow or auction algorithms, or greedy with local improvements when time-bounded.

362
00:29:23.214 --> 00:29:26.755
Batches are per area, so n stays small (tens to hundreds).

363
00:29:28.105 --> 00:29:33.119
Batch window trade-off: longer windows give better global matches but add waiting.

364
00:29:33.399 --> 00:29:36.940
2 to 5 s is typical, tuned per city by experiment.

365
00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:41.977
Look-ahead: include drivers finishing trips within ~2 min near the request.

366
00:29:42.257 --> 00:29:44.945
Their availability time is added to the ETA.

367
00:29:45.225 --> 00:29:51.199
Fairness terms: spread offers among waiting drivers (idle time bonus) to avoid starving some.

368
00:29:52.549 --> 00:29:53.637
Routing and ETA.

369
00:29:53.917 --> 00:29:54.983
Shortest paths:

370
00:29:55.263 --> 00:29:57.077
Dijkstra and A are the basis.

371
00:29:57.357 --> 00:30:03.778
Contraction hierarchies add shortcut edges in preprocessing so queries explore only upward nodes.

372
00:30:04.058 --> 00:30:06.447
That is millisecond queries on city graphs.

373
00:30:06.727 --> 00:30:13.490
Customisable variants (CCH or CRP) allow traffic weight updates without full re-preprocessing.

374
00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:22.474
Traffic weights: per-segment speed estimates from recent traces (streaming aggregation), blended with the historical time-of-day profile.

375
00:30:23.824 --> 00:30:29.499
ETA correction: a model predicts the residual between routing-engine ETA and actual time.

376
00:30:29.779 --> 00:30:33.384
Features: route, time, weather, pickup or drop-off type, city.

377
00:30:33.664 --> 00:30:35.520
It is trained on completed trips.

378
00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:38.107
Dynamic pricing.

379
00:30:38.387 --> 00:30:43.614
The goal is to keep the market in balance: requests fulfilled with reasonable pickup times.

380
00:30:43.894 --> 00:31:05.334
A simple, explainable version: per pricing zone, compute imbalance = forecast demand or available supply over the next minutes;. map imbalance to a multiplier through a monotonic curve with caps;. smooth it over time (exponential smoothing or rate limits) and space (neighbour averaging), to avoid flicker and zone-boundary gaming.

381
00:31:06.684 --> 00:31:24.839
More advanced versions: estimate demand elasticity (how many riders still request at each price) and supply elasticity (how many drivers move or stay online);. choose prices to maximise completed trips or a welfare objective, subject to caps and fairness constraints.

382
00:31:25.119 --> 00:31:34.164
Guard rails: regulatory caps (VERIFY), emergency overrides, consistency between quote and charge, and audit logs of every multiplier.

383
00:31:34.444 --> 00:31:35.489
Evaluation:

384
00:31:36.839 --> 00:31:51.325
switchback experiments (alternate policies over time slots per city) avoid interference between treatment and control riders, who share the same drivers;. metrics: completion rate, wait time, cancellations, earnings per hour.

385
00:31:52.675 --> 00:31:53.720
Map matching.

386
00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:01.381
The problem: raw GPS points are noisy (±10 to 30 m in cities, worse near tall buildings).

387
00:32:01.661 --> 00:32:04.456
The trip must be placed on the actual road sequence.

388
00:32:04.736 --> 00:32:11.093
Hidden Markov model: hidden states: candidate road segments near each point;. emission probability:

389
00:32:11.373 --> 00:32:21.784
Gaussian on the distance from point to segment;. transition probability: how plausible the move between segments is, comparing route distance with straight-line distance;.

390
00:32:23.134 --> 00:32:27.507
solving: the Viterbi algorithm finds the most likely segment sequence.

391
00:32:27.787 --> 00:32:34.102
Outputs: accurate distance for fares, road positions for ETA and traffic, and detection of detours.

392
00:32:35.452 --> 00:32:36.689
Pooling insertion.

393
00:32:36.969 --> 00:32:47.209
Insertion: for a new shared request, for each candidate trip, try inserting the pickup at position i and the drop-off at position j i in the stop sequence.

394
00:32:47.489 --> 00:32:53.975
Check that: capacity is respected;. every existing rider's detour is within limits;. time windows are met.

395
00:32:54.255 --> 00:32:57.028
Choose the insertion with the smallest added cost.

396
00:32:57.308 --> 00:33:03.025
This is insertion heuristics for the dial-a-ride problem, with local search to improve sequences.

397
00:33:04.375 --> 00:33:10.434
Pruning: only trips whose route passes near the new pickup, found by a geo index on route corridors.

398
00:33:11.784 --> 00:33:13.789
Supply positioning and incentives.

399
00:33:14.069 --> 00:33:17.397
Heat maps show forecast demand minus supply per cell.

400
00:33:17.677 --> 00:33:25.549
Incentive targeting: allocate budget to zones and times with the largest forecast gaps and the highest expected response.

401
00:33:25.829 --> 00:33:27.920
Uplift is modelled from experiments.

402
00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:35.944
Repositioning suggestions: each idle driver gets a short list of nearby cells with the best expected earnings per hour.

403
00:33:36.224 --> 00:33:41.472
Suggestions are randomised across drivers, so everyone doesn't converge on the same cell.

404
00:33:42.822 --> 00:33:43.910
Fraud detection.

405
00:33:44.190 --> 00:33:55.625
GPS spoofing: rules on physics (speed, acceleration), consistency (GPS versus network location, sensor data), and device integrity attestations.

406
00:33:55.905 --> 00:34:05.697
Collusion: a graph of rider, driver pairs, devices and payment instruments; clusters with unusual repeated interactions and incentive-heavy patterns.

407
00:34:05.977 --> 00:34:11.523
Community detection (for example, connected components, Louvain) plus supervised models.

408
00:34:12.873 --> 00:34:14.879
ETA and fare quality monitoring.

409
00:34:15.159 --> 00:34:16.375
Accuracy tracking:

410
00:34:16.655 --> 00:34:21.945
ETA error distributions per city and hour; fare quote versus final fare differences.

411
00:34:22.225 --> 00:34:28.924
Alerting: alerts when error drifts, which may indicate map data issues, traffic feed failures or model drift.

412
00:34:30.274 --> 00:34:31.213
Part 7.

413
00:34:31.493 --> 00:34:32.815
Data Architecture.

414
00:34:34.065 --> 00:34:35.452
Operational stores.

415
00:34:35.732 --> 00:34:37.097
Here is a quick table:

416
00:34:37.377 --> 00:34:39.553
Data, and Store (reference), and Notes.

417
00:34:39.833 --> 00:34:42.031
Live driver locations and status.

418
00:34:42.311 --> 00:34:43.548
Store (reference):

419
00:34:43.828 --> 00:34:46.985
In-memory sharded service (Redis Cluster or custom).

420
00:34:47.265 --> 00:34:48.076
Notes:

421
00:34:48.356 --> 00:34:50.169
Ephemeral; rebuilt from pings.

422
00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:51.217
Trips.

423
00:34:51.497 --> 00:34:52.735
Store (reference):

424
00:34:53.015 --> 00:35:00.524
Sharded relational (MySQL or PostgreSQL, Vitess or Citus) or a distributed SQL database.

425
00:35:00.804 --> 00:35:01.615
Notes:

426
00:35:01.895 --> 00:35:05.521
Per-trip strong consistency; sharded by city or trip id.

427
00:35:05.801 --> 00:35:08.340
Riders, drivers, vehicles, documents.

428
00:35:08.620 --> 00:35:09.857
Store (reference):

429
00:35:10.137 --> 00:35:12.868
Relational + object storage for documents.

430
00:35:13.148 --> 00:35:13.959
Notes:

431
00:35:14.239 --> 00:35:15.604
PII encrypted.

432
00:35:15.884 --> 00:35:16.652
Ledger.

433
00:35:16.932 --> 00:35:18.169
Store (reference):

434
00:35:18.449 --> 00:35:20.625
Relational with double-entry constraints.

435
00:35:20.905 --> 00:35:21.716
Notes:

436
00:35:21.996 --> 00:35:24.257
Ch 45, I03.

437
00:35:24.537 --> 00:35:25.497
Fare quotes.

438
00:35:25.777 --> 00:35:27.015
Store (reference):

439
00:35:27.295 --> 00:35:28.724
Key-value with TTL.

440
00:35:29.004 --> 00:35:29.815
Notes:

441
00:35:30.095 --> 00:35:31.908
Quote IDs referenced by trips.

442
00:35:33.258 --> 00:35:34.709
Maps and routing data.

443
00:35:34.989 --> 00:35:36.226
