612
00:45:53.601 --> 00:45:54.540
Part 12.

613
00:45:54.820 --> 00:45:56.164
Interview Q and A.

614
00:45:57.414 --> 00:45:58.246
Beginner.

615
00:45:58.526 --> 00:45:59.550
Question 1.

616
00:45:59.830 --> 00:46:03.009
Walk me through what happens when a rider requests a ride.

617
00:46:03.289 --> 00:46:04.654
Thirty-second answer:

618
00:46:04.934 --> 00:46:24.902
Fare estimate (route, ETA, price with multiplier), then request creates a trip with the quote, then matching assigns a driver in the next batch, locking the driver, then offer to driver, then accept, then navigation to pickup, arrival, PIN to start, then live tracking, then end and final fare, then payment, ledger, ratings.

619
00:46:26.252 --> 00:46:27.255
Question 2.

620
00:46:27.535 --> 00:46:29.561
How do you find drivers near a rider?

621
00:46:29.841 --> 00:46:31.207
Thirty-second answer:

622
00:46:31.487 --> 00:46:44.735
Keep live driver positions in an in-memory geo index sharded by cell (for example, H3 hexagons); search the rider's cell and neighbouring rings, then compute route ETAs for the closest candidates only.

623
00:46:46.085 --> 00:46:47.130
Question 3.

624
00:46:47.410 --> 00:46:49.629
How does the app show the car moving on the map?

625
00:46:49.909 --> 00:46:51.274
Thirty-second answer:

626
00:46:51.554 --> 00:47:02.541
The driver app sends GPS every few seconds; the server map-matches it to roads and pushes it to the rider over a persistent connection; the rider app animates between points.

627
00:47:03.891 --> 00:47:04.957
Question 4.

628
00:47:05.237 --> 00:47:08.011
Why does the driver ask for a PIN at the start?

629
00:47:08.291 --> 00:47:09.656
Thirty-second answer:

630
00:47:09.936 --> 00:47:17.509
To confirm the right rider got into the right car, preventing wrong pickups and some fraud, and to mark the trip start reliably.

631
00:47:18.859 --> 00:47:19.862
Intermediate.

632
00:47:20.142 --> 00:47:21.251
Question 5.

633
00:47:21.531 --> 00:47:23.409
How would you design surge pricing?

634
00:47:23.689 --> 00:47:25.054
Thirty-second answer:

635
00:47:25.334 --> 00:47:43.041
Forecast demand and supply per zone for the next minutes, map the imbalance to a multiplier with a monotonic curve, smooth over time and space, apply caps (including regulatory ones) and emergency overrides, show it in the upfront fare, and evaluate with switchback experiments.

636
00:47:44.391 --> 00:47:45.500
Question 6.

637
00:47:45.780 --> 00:47:48.639
How are upfront fares calculated and kept honest?

638
00:47:48.919 --> 00:47:50.284
Thirty-second answer:

639
00:47:50.564 --> 00:48:08.932
From route distance and time, fees, tolls, multiplier, promotions and taxes; stored as a quote with inputs; the final fare equals the quote unless the rider changed the trip (destination, stops, waiting), reconciliation compares actual versus quote and auto-resolves disputes.

640
00:48:10.282 --> 00:48:11.370
Question 7.

641
00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:14.615
How do you prevent a driver from being assigned two trips?

642
00:48:14.895 --> 00:48:16.261
Thirty-second answer:

643
00:48:16.541 --> 00:48:29.874
Driver state is changed only with compare-and-set (available, then offered, then assigned) in a strongly consistent store; matchers are partitioned by area; offers time out and release the driver explicitly.

644
00:48:31.224 --> 00:48:32.248
Question 8.

645
00:48:32.528 --> 00:48:34.448
How do cash trips work in the ledger?

646
00:48:34.728 --> 00:48:36.093
Thirty-second answer:

647
00:48:36.373 --> 00:48:48.853
The rider pays the driver; the driver confirms; the platform's commission becomes a receivable from the driver, netted against digital earnings or collected via top-up; everything posts as double-entry.

648
00:48:50.203 --> 00:48:51.270
Question 9.

649
00:48:51.550 --> 00:48:54.131
How do airport queues differ from normal matching?

650
00:48:54.411 --> 00:48:55.777
Thirty-second answer:

651
00:48:56.057 --> 00:49:10.905
The airport is a geofenced zone with a FIFO driver queue per product; requests from the terminal go to the first eligible driver in the queue, not the nearest; leaving the zone forfeits the place; short trips may get queue priority on return.

652
00:49:12.255 --> 00:49:13.023
Senior.

653
00:49:13.303 --> 00:49:14.348
Question 10.

654
00:49:14.628 --> 00:49:18.105
Why batch matching instead of nearest driver, and how does it work?

655
00:49:18.385 --> 00:49:19.751
Thirty-second answer:

656
00:49:20.031 --> 00:49:39.679
Greedy nearest-driver is locally optimal but globally poor; batching requests and drivers every few seconds and solving a min-cost bipartite matching (Hungarian or min-cost flow) on ETA plus penalties lowers average pickup times; include drivers finishing trips nearby; tune the window per city.

657
00:49:41.029 --> 00:49:42.159
Question 11.

658
00:49:42.439 --> 00:49:44.167
How do you compute accurate ETAs?

659
00:49:44.447 --> 00:49:45.813
Thirty-second answer:

660
00:49:46.093 --> 00:50:03.671
A road graph with turn restrictions, live segment speeds from driver traces blended with historical profiles, fast shortest paths (contraction hierarchies), and an ML model that corrects the routing ETA using features like time, weather and pickup type, monitored for error drift.

661
00:50:05.021 --> 00:50:06.109
Question 12.

662
00:50:06.389 --> 00:50:07.947
How does map matching work?

663
00:50:08.227 --> 00:50:09.592
Thirty-second answer:

664
00:50:09.872 --> 00:50:25.829
A hidden Markov model: candidate road segments near each GPS point are states, emission probabilities come from distance to segment, transition probabilities from route versus straight-line distance, and Viterbi finds the most likely road sequence.

665
00:50:27.179 --> 00:50:28.459
Question 13.

666
00:50:28.739 --> 00:50:30.595
How would you implement shared rides?

667
00:50:30.875 --> 00:50:32.241
Thirty-second answer:

668
00:50:32.521 --> 00:50:48.926
For a new request, find candidate trips whose routes pass near the pickup, try insertion positions for pickup and drop-off, check capacity, detour limits and time windows, and choose the cheapest insertion; fix each rider's fare upfront; re-plan the driver's route.

669
00:50:50.276 --> 00:50:51.471
Question 14.

670
00:50:51.751 --> 00:50:54.524
How do you detect GPS spoofing and collusion?

671
00:50:54.804 --> 00:50:56.169
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:50:56.449 --> 00:51:15.543
Spoofing via physics checks (speed, jumps), mock-location and device integrity signals, GPS versus network location consistency; collusion via graphs of rider, driver pairs, devices and payments with community detection plus supervised models; act on incentives and accounts.

673
00:51:16.893 --> 00:51:17.789
Architect.

674
00:51:18.069 --> 00:51:19.263
Question 15.

675
00:51:19.543 --> 00:51:22.338
Design the data platform for a ride-hailing company.

676
00:51:22.618 --> 00:51:23.983
Thirty-second answer:

677
00:51:24.263 --> 00:51:46.322
Kafka for all events; Flink for real-time features and aggregates feeding marketplace engines and a real-time OLAP store (Pinot) for ops dashboards; a Hudi or Iceberg lakehouse for trips, offers, quotes and payments with late updates; Presto or Trino and Spark for analysis; a feature store and ML platform; strict governance for location data.

678
00:51:47.672 --> 00:51:48.867
Question 16.

679
00:51:49.147 --> 00:51:52.048
How do you experiment on pricing or matching fairly?

680
00:51:52.328 --> 00:51:53.693
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:51:53.973 --> 00:52:12.555
Riders and drivers share one market, so user-level A or B tests interfere; use switchback designs (alternate policies across time slots per city) or geo-randomisation, with enough slots, pre-registered metrics (completion, wait, cancellations, driver earnings) and variance reduction.

682
00:52:13.905 --> 00:52:15.249
Question 17.

683
00:52:15.529 --> 00:52:18.771
How would you make a city's marketplace resilient to failures?

684
00:52:19.051 --> 00:52:20.417
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:52:20.697 --> 00:52:37.678
Partition by city with independent deployments, strongly consistent replicated trip store, apps that continue trips offline, graceful fallbacks (fixed multiplier, greedy matching, no pooling), multi-region failover for connections, and per-city funnel monitoring.

686
00:52:39.028 --> 00:52:40.201
Question 18.

687
00:52:40.481 --> 00:52:44.151
How do regulations shape a ride-hailing platform in India?

688
00:52:44.431 --> 00:52:45.796
Thirty-second answer:

689
00:52:46.076 --> 00:53:07.025
Aggregator licensing and guidelines set rules on driver verification, vehicle fitness, fare structure and surge limits, safety features, grievance handling and data localisation, with state-level variations, so pricing caps, onboarding checks and data residency must be configurable per jurisdiction (VERIFY current texts).

690
00:53:08.375 --> 00:53:09.613
Question 19.

691
00:53:09.893 --> 00:53:13.562
How would you design driver incentives without wasting budget?

692
00:53:13.842 --> 00:53:15.207
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:53:15.487 --> 00:53:31.573
Target incentives to forecast supply gaps by zone and time, segment drivers by expected response, measure uplift against control groups, cap budgets, randomise repositioning suggestions to avoid herding, and account for incentives in the ledger.

694
00:53:32.923 --> 00:53:34.032
Question 20.

695
00:53:34.312 --> 00:53:38.515
Compare ride-hailing dispatch with food or grocery delivery dispatch.

696
00:53:38.795 --> 00:53:40.160
Thirty-second answer:

697
00:53:40.440 --> 00:54:02.243
Both match moving couriers to jobs with ETAs; rides have a single pickup-drop-off with the customer present and price elasticity at request time, while delivery adds preparation time uncertainty, batching multiple orders per courier and store-side waits, so delivery dispatch optimises over longer horizons with batching (Ch 47).

698
00:54:03.593 --> 00:54:04.638
Part 13.

699
00:54:04.918 --> 00:54:05.686
Summary.

700
00:54:06.936 --> 00:54:16.600
Ride-hailing is a real-time, local market: partitioned by city and hexagonal cell, with ephemeral in-memory location state and durable trip state.

701
00:54:16.880 --> 00:54:20.059
Matching is batched optimisation, not nearest-driver.

702
00:54:20.339 --> 00:54:25.352
Pricing balances the market with smoothed, capped multipliers tested by switchbacks.

703
00:54:25.632 --> 00:54:33.568
Maps are the hidden core: road graphs, fast routing, live traffic from driver traces, ML-corrected ETAs, HMM map matching.

704
00:54:33.848 --> 00:54:43.640
Money and safety need strong guarantees: idempotent trips, a double-entry ledger with cash receivables, SOS and anomaly detection, fraud graphs.

705
00:54:44.990 --> 00:54:46.291
The data platform:

706
00:54:46.571 --> 00:54:56.171
Kafka, then Flink, then real-time OLAP for operations; lakehouse (Hudi), then SQL and ML for everything else; strict location privacy.

